    "content": "NATO (North Atlantic Treaty Organization) is an intergovernmental military alliance established in 1949 to guarantee the security of its member states through collective defense. Founded to counter Soviet influence, it now includes 32 nations (principally in Europe and North America) committed to mutual defense. The alliance acts as a peacekeeping organization in the Euro-Atlantic area. Key Aspects of NATO: Definition: Founded on April 4, 1949, by the North Atlantic Treaty (Washington Treaty). Purpose: To promote stability, security, and freedom for its members, primarily through collective defense (Article 5). Article 5: The principle that an armed attack against one member is considered an attack against all members. Members: 32 member states as of 2024, including the US, UK, Canada, and most of Europe. Headquarters: Brussels, Belgium. Usage: Used in political and military contexts to describe peacekeeping initiatives, security pacts, and joint exercises (e.g., \"NATO alliance,\" \"NATO summit,\" \"NATO intervention\"). Synonyms/Alternatives: North Atlantic Alliance, The Atlantic Alliance, Western Alliance, or OTAN (Organisation du traité de l'Atlantique nord in French). Usage Examples: \"The NATO alliance has expanded to 32 members.\" \"Article 5 was triggered for the first time after the 9/11 attacks.\" \"The NATO headquarters is located in Brussels.\"",
